<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kaleb</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 514–542 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as King Kaleb</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kaleb was a king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksumite-empire">Aksum</a> who reigned c. 514–542 CE. He led a naval expedition across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> that defeated the Himyarite king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhu-nuwas">Dhu Nuwas</a> and placed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-arabia">South Arabia</a> under Aksumite control. His reign marks the furthest extent of the Aksumite Empire's military reach.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kaleb.jpg" alt="Kaleb" width="320" height="158" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Classical Numismatic Group · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kaleb.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Yemen campaigns of <b>King Kaleb</b> (r. c. 514–542 CE) marked the fullest extension of Aksumite reach: a major seaborne expedition around 525, undertaken with the encouragement of the Byzantine Emperor Justin I, overthrew the Himyarite king Dhu Nuwas, who had persecuted the Christians of Najran, and installed…</blockquote><a href="/empire/aksumite-empire">Aksumite Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 520s the kingdom's military capacity proved insufficient: <b>Kaleb of Aksum</b>, with Byzantine encouragement, carried an army across the Red Sea in 525 and defeated and killed Yusuf As'ar Yath'ar.</blockquote><a href="/empire/himyarite-kingdom">Himyarite Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Six Ge'ez inscriptions are known from 6th-century South Arabia, from the reign of <b>Kaleb</b> of Axum, the king who conquered Himyar; they record his conquests, name Jesus, Mary and David and quote Isaiah, the Psalms and Matthew.</blockquote><a href="/language/geez">Ge'ez · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>In 525 the Aksumite Empire under <b>King Kaleb</b> crossed the Red Sea to avenge the Christians of Najran, defeated the Himyarite king Yusuf and installed Sumyafa Ashwa, a local Christian, as viceroy.</blockquote><a href="/state/aksumite-yemen">Aksumite Yemen</a></li></ul></section>
